@namespace ClimateExplorer.Web.Client.Components.RecentObservations

<div class="trends-overview">
    <p>
        A trend here is a simple (ordinary least squares) linear regression: the straight line
        through a set of yearly values that minimises the sum of the squared vertical distances
        from each year's value to the line. See
        <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Simple_linear_regression" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Wikipedia: Simple linear regression</a>
        for the full mathematical background.
    </p>

    <p>
        <strong>Why rates are shown per decade.</strong> The underlying calculation works in whatever
        units the data uses - degrees per year, millimetres per year - but a per-year number is small
        enough to look like noise. Multiplying by ten to get a per-decade rate makes the number large
        enough to read at a glance.
    </p>

    <p>
        <strong>Why some trends say "No significant trend" instead of a number.</strong> Every slope
        estimated from real, noisy data comes with uncertainty - a p-value, the probability of seeing
        a slope this far from zero by chance alone if the true long-term trend were actually zero.
        This site treats a slope as significant when that p-value is below 0.05.
        When a trend doesn't clear that bar, showing a specific number such as
        "+0.08°C per decade" would overstate confidence the data doesn't support, so the tile shows
        "No significant trend" instead.
    </p>

    <p>
        <strong>How the slope itself is worked out.</strong> Picture each year in the record as a point:
        <em>x</em> is the year, <em>y</em> is that year's value (temperature, rainfall, whatever the
        metric is). The line's slope, written β ("beta"), comes from this formula:
    </p>

    <SlopeFormulaFigure />

    <p>
        Working through it: for every year, take how far that year is from the average year (x − x̄),
        and how far that year's value is from the average value (y − ȳ), and multiply the two together.
        Add up that product for every year in the record - that's the sum on top. The sum on the bottom
        does the same thing with just the years: how far each year is from the average year, squared,
        and added up across the whole record. Divide one sum by the other and the result is the slope -
        how much the fitted line rises for each extra year.
    </p>

    <p>
        <strong>A small worked example.</strong> Say a station has three years of data: 2001 at 14.00°C,
        2002 at 14.05°C, 2003 at 14.10°C. The average year is 2002 and the average temperature is 14.05°C.
    </p>

    <table class="trends-slope-example">
        <thead>
            <tr>
                <th scope="col">Year (x)</th>
                <th scope="col">Temp °C (y)</th>
                <th scope="col">x − x̄</th>
                <th scope="col">y − ȳ</th>
                <th scope="col">(x−x̄)(y−ȳ)</th>
                <th scope="col">(x−x̄)²</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr><td>2001</td><td>14.00</td><td>−1</td><td>−0.05</td><td>0.05</td><td>1</td></tr>
            <tr><td>2002</td><td>14.05</td><td>0</td><td>0.00</td><td>0.00</td><td>0</td></tr>
            <tr><td>2003</td><td>14.10</td><td>1</td><td>0.05</td><td>0.05</td><td>1</td></tr>
            <tr class="trends-slope-example-sum"><td colspan="4">Sum</td><td>0.10</td><td>2</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>

    <p>
        The slope is the sum on top divided by the sum on the bottom: 0.10 ÷ 2 = 0.05°C per year - the
        +0.5°C per decade the tile would display, once that per-year figure is multiplied by ten as
        described next.
    </p>

    <p>
        <strong>What's on these pages.</strong> The tile's own rows only ever show the per-decade
        rate, or "No significant trend" - nothing else. R², the p-value, confidence intervals, the
        fitted equation, and everything else in a full statistical breakdown are only available here,
        one metric and time window at a time.
    </p>
</div>
